      "content": "While no event happens without a cause, microscopic environmental fluctuations can create \"stochastic\" jumps where one strain suddenly overpowers another if the overall conditions remain within a functional range. Sourdough rarely fails for no reason; bad smells or explosive gas production are almost always traceable back to a specific trigger, such as abandoned starter factors that create heat pockets, hidden black mold that eats nutrients, or a failure to feed the colonies just before their peak. Essentially, while the exact moment of chaos can seem random, it is always the direct result of a measurable shift in the microbial community or its surrounding environment.",
